 # Radiance-field optimization
 
-Also called: Differentiable radiance field training, Inverse radiance field optimization
+Also called: Differentiable radiance field optimization, Inverse radiance field rendering
 
 ## Overview
-Radiance-field optimization is the family of methods that reconstruct 3D scene representations (radiance fields) from 2D observations by minimizing the photometric error between rendered images and ground-truth inputs. It solves the inverse problem of determining scene parameters (density, color, geometry, or camera poses) such that the differentiable volume rendering equation produces images matching the input data. It sets itself apart from explicit forward rendering methods by treating the scene representation as a set of trainable parameters optimized via gradient descent, and from tomographic reconstruction by relying on the differentiability of the rendering operator rather than algebraic inversion of projection data.
+Radiance-field optimization is the family of inverse rendering methods that reconstruct a 3D scene representation by minimizing the photometric error between rendered images and ground-truth observations [S2]. It solves the problem of novel view synthesis and scene reconstruction by treating the scene parameters (density, color, geometry, or neural weights) as differentiable variables optimized via gradient descent [S2]. Unlike explicit mesh-based reconstruction, radiance-field optimization relies on the differentiability of the forward volume rendering operator to propagate gradients from pixel space to the scene representation [44-radiance-field-optimization.md]. It sets itself apart from sibling methods like [Tomographic reconstruction as inverse rendering](45-tomographic-reconstruction-as-inverse-rendering.md) by typically using continuous or semi-continuous neural or voxel-based representations rather than discrete algebraic reconstruction techniques, and from [Differentiable volume rendering](43-differentiable-volume-rendering.md) by encompassing both the forward rendering model and the specific optimization strategies (e.g., adaptive sampling, hybrid representations) tailored for radiance fields [S2].
 
 ## Shared principles
-All methods in this family rely on the differentiability of the forward rendering operator and the propagation of gradients through the integration steps. The core mechanism is the evaluation of the volume rendering equation and the computation of gradients with respect to scene parameters (density, geometry, transfer functions, camera poses).
