+Neural Volumes (NV) is a method for synthesizing novel views of dynamic scenes by optimizing a deep 3D convolutional network to predict a discretized RGB$\alpha$ voxel grid and a 3D warp field. It solves the problem of rendering semi-transparent, dynamic objects (such as smoke or hair) from sparse multi-view video inputs without requiring explicit geometry reconstruction or depth sensors. Unlike implicit neural radiance fields that map coordinates to color/density via MLPs, NV uses an explicit voxel grid, trading memory for renderable structure and enabling real-time inference at the cost of fixed resolution limits. It sets itself apart from static voxel methods like DeepVoxels by incorporating a learned warp field to model non-rigid motion and from MLP-based methods by maintaining an interpretable, differentiable volumetric representation.
 
