+### [Direct Voxel Grid Optimization](4694-direct-voxel-grid-optimization.md)
+Direct Voxel Grid Optimization (DVG) reconstructs per-scene radiance fields from calibrated images by directly optimizing a dense 3D voxel grid for density and a feature voxel grid for color, achieving convergence in less than 15 minutes on a single GPU. Unlike [Neural Radiance Fields](4972-neural-radiance-fields.md) which uses an implicit MLP, DVG uses explicit discretized volumes, and unlike prior voxel methods that convert from trained implicit models, it trains the voxel grid directly from scratch without cross-scene pretraining. It distinguishes itself from siblings like [Point-NeRF](6569-point-nerf.md) by using a dense grid with post-activation interpolation to produce sharp surfaces at lower resolutions, and from [MVSNeRF](6628-volume-feature-rendering.md) by achieving higher PSNR without requiring generalizable pre-training.
 
 ### [Neural Transient Fields (NeTF)](4760-neural-transient-fields-netf.md)
-Models the plenoptic transient field using an MLP to predict transient arrival times from sparse SPAD measurements for Non-Line-of-Sight imaging.
+Neural Transient Fields (NeTF) is a method for Non-Line-of-Sight (NLOS) imaging that models the plenoptic transient field using a Multi-Layer Perceptron (MLP) to predict transient arrival times $\tau$ from sparse Single-Photon Avalanche Diode (SPAD) measurements on a relay wall. Unlike [NeRF](4972-neural-radiance-fields.md), which relies on dense high-resolution images and 1D ray sampling, NeTF operates on sparse 2D hemispherical spot measurements, making occlusion handling and sampling bias critical challenges.
 
 ### [Neural Radiance Fields](4972-neural-radiance-fields.md)
-Synthesizes novel views by learning a continuous function (typically an MLP) that maps spatial coordinates and viewing directions to volume density and emitted color, representing the scene implicitly.
+Neural Radiance Fields (NeRF) represent a 3D scene as a continuous volumetric function that maps 3D spatial coordinates and 2D viewing directions to emitted RGB color and volume density. It solves the problem of novel view synthesis by replacing explicit geometric primitives (meshes or point clouds) with an implicit neural representation encoded in network weights, enabling photorealistic rendering of complex scenes with view-dependent effects like specularities and transparency. It distinguishes itself from its parent method [Radiance-field optimization](44-radiance-field-optimization.md) by introducing specific architectural choices (such as asymmetric MLPs or hierarchical sampling) and by serving as the foundational implicit representation for a wide variety of extensions including dynamic scenes, generative models, and hybrid explicit-implicit structures.
 
