+### [NeRF++](6198-nerf.md)
+NeRF++ (also known as NeRF--) is a framework for novel view synthesis that jointly optimizes camera parameters (intrinsics and 6DoF poses) and the scene representation (NeRF) from a sparse set of forward-facing RGB images, eliminating the need for pre-computed camera parameters via Structure-from-Motion (SfM). Unlike standard NeRF which assumes known camera poses, NeRF++ treats camera parameters as learnable variables optimized end-to-end alongside the neural radiance field. It is specifically designed for forward-facing scenes and differs from concurrent methods that either require intrinsics as input or assume a pre-trained NeRF.
 
 ### [HybridNeRF](6363-hybridnerf.md)
-Uses a hybrid surface–volume representation that renders most of the scene as surfaces while modeling challenging regions volumetrically using spatially-varying surfaceness parameters.
+HybridNeRF is a neural rendering method that combines the efficiency of surface-based representations with the flexibility of volumetric rendering to achieve high-fidelity novel-view synthesis at real-time frame rates. It solves the trade-off between the slow sampling required by pure volumetric NeRFs and the inability of pure surface methods to model thin, semi-opaque, or translucent structures. Unlike siblings such as [Surface NeRF](7385-surface-nerf.md) which use a global surfaceness parameter, HybridNeRF learns a spatially-varying surfaceness field $\beta(\mathbf{x})$, allowing it to render most of the scene as thin surfaces (using few samples) while modeling challenging regions volumetrically.
 
 ### [Ref-DVGO](6572-ref-dvgo.md)
-Combines the fast hybrid implicit-explicit representation of DVGO with reflection direction reparameterization to achieve high-quality photorealistic novel view synthesis of reflective scenes.
+Ref-DVGO is a reflection-aware neural rendering method that combines the fast hybrid implicit-explicit representation of Direct Voxel Grid Optimization (DVGO) with the reflection direction reparameterization of Ref-NeRF to achieve high-quality photorealistic novel view synthesis of reflective scenes with reduced training and rendering time. It replaces the large spatial Multi-Layer Perceptrons (MLPs) of Ref-NeRF with six distinct voxel grids, significantly reducing GPU memory requirements and training duration while maintaining competitive quality for shiny objects.
 
-### Local Light Field Fusion
-Designed for producing photorealistic novel views for well-sampled forward-facing scenes. It uses a trained 3D convolutional network to directly predict a discretized frustum-sampled RGB$\alpha$ grid (multiplane image or MPI) for each input view, then renders novel views by alpha compositing and blending nearby MPIs into the novel viewpoint. It does not exceed 64 pixels of disparity between input views. It can process a small input dataset in under 10 minutes but produces enormous storage requirements (over 15GB for one "Realistic Synthetic" scene). It frequently fails to estimate correct geometry in datasets with up to 400-500 pixels of disparity between views and blends between different scene representations for rendering different views, resulting in perceptually-distracting inconsistency [S5].
+### [Neural Radiosity](6755-neural-radiosity.md)
+Neural Radiosity (NR) is an inverse rendering algorithm that represents the radiance function $L(\mathbf{x}, \omega_o)$ as a single neural network $L_\phi(\mathbf{x}, \omega_o)$. It solves the rendering equation by directly optimizing the network parameters to minimize the $L_2$ norm of the residual of the rendering equation. Unlike methods that render images and compare them to ground truth, NR minimizes the physical inconsistency of the radiance field itself. It is distinguished from its siblings by its self-training approach on the residual rather than on pixel errors, and by its reliance on automatic differentiation frameworks (like Mitsuba2) to compute gradients of the residual with respect to scene parameters.
 
-### Inverse Rendering with Adjoint Derivatives
-An optimization method that estimates scene optical properties (position, scale, rotation, cross-section, spherical harmonic coefficients) from images using gradient-based optimization with adjoint derivatives of the forward pass. It starts optimization with a small number of primitives and gradually generates new primitives to add detail. It uses the Bound Adam optimizer (an improved Adam optimizer that respects parameter boundaries) and a loss function consisting of L1 error, D-SSIM, plus regularization terms for anisotropy and density of small primitives [S6, unofficial].
+### [Optimization Procedures](7150-optimization-procedures.md)
+Optimization Procedures define the iterative algorithms and training dynamics used to minimize the photometric error between rendered images and ground-truth observations by updating the parameters of differentiable volume or radiance fields. This family focuses on the optimization dynamics—specifically how rays are sampled, how gradients are propagated, and how hyperparameters are scheduled—rather than the underlying field representations themselves [5622-optimization-loops-and-training-procedures.md]. It addresses the ill-posed nature of inverse volume rendering by balancing reconstruction fidelity against structural constraints, often employing auxiliary regularization losses [1413-regularization-losses.md].
