+### Hybrid Representation
+Hybrid Representation is a framework combining explicit feature storage and an implicit decoder to efficiently reconstruct and render radiance fields, addressing the computational expense of pure implicit NeRF [S8]. The steps are: 1. Store explicit features $V$ (e.g., voxel grid, hash table, basis vectors). 2. For any point $p$, efficiently obtain feature vector $v_p = V(p)$ (e.g., tri-linear interpolation). 3. Use a decoder $D$ (MLP or spherical harmonics) to map $v_p$ to density $\sigma$ and color $c$ [S8]. The Explicit Feature Storage $V$ can be a voxel grid, hash table, or set of basis vectors/matrices [S8]. The Decoder $D$ can be an MLP or spherical harmonics [S8].
+
+### Per-Scene Fine-tuning
+Per-Scene Fine-tuning is a post-processing step where the predicted voxel features of the global radiance field are optimized per scene to boost rendering quality [S9]. The steps are: 1. Take the global radiance field $V_g$ predicted by the network. 2. Optimize the predicted voxel features per scene using rendering losses for a short period [S9]. Fine-tuning takes approximately 20 minutes to 1 hour, resulting in state-of-the-art quality that outperforms NeRF [S9].
+
+### Nerflets
+Nerflets achieves efficient structure-aware 3D scene representation with only 2D supervision by representing a scene as a set of local neural radiance fields (Nerflets), each with orientation, spatial position, and range [S10]. For a sampled point, N small MLPs generate N densities and colors, one for each Nerflet [S10]. The final color and density of the sampled point are obtained through weighted averaging based on the influence weights of each Nerflet [S10]. On KITTI-360 and ScanNet datasets, PSNR is more than one point higher [S10].
+
+### DP-NeRF
+DP-NeRF is a method that introduces physical scene priors to handle challenging scenes with blur and exposure, utilizing point cloud 3D priors to synthesize images due to the sparsity of point cloud representation [S10].
+
+### General Radiance Field Rendering via MLP
+General Radiance Field Rendering via MLP is an MLP that predicts RGB values and volumetric density for a 3D point using its aggregated feature vector and viewing direction, forming a general radiance field [S11]. The steps are: Render an image pixel RGB by casting a ray from the camera center and estimating the integral using volume rendering equations with numerical quadrature [S11]. Estimate the integrals with numerical quadrature using a hierarchical sampling strategy, strictly following NeRF [S11]. All 3D locations and RGB values are processed by the positional encoding proposed by NeRF [S11]. Optimization uses L2 loss between rendered RGBs and ground truth [S11]. Unlike original NeRF which uses point position xyz as input, this method uses the aggregated point features $\bar{F}_p$ as input to the MLP [S11].
+
+### Baking Implicit NeRF into Multi-resolution Voxel Mesh
+Baking Implicit NeRF into Multi-resolution Voxel Mesh is a technique to replace the computational bottleneck of MLP inference in implicit NeRF by pre-calculating voxel properties (color and density) into a multi-resolution voxel grid structure [S12]. The steps are: Spatial division: Divide scene space into a three-level voxel grid based on adaptive resolution. Define voxel side length $s_k = \frac{S}{2^k}$ where $S$ is the maximum side length of the scene and $k=0, 1, 2$ correspond to LOD0 (512³), LOD1 (256³), and LOD2 (128³) [S12].
 
 ## Background
-Radiance-field optimization emerged as a dominant paradigm in computer graphics and vision for novel view synthesis and inverse rendering. The foundational work on Neural Radiance Fields (NeRF) by Mildenhall et al. (2021) demonstrated the efficacy of learning continuous volumetric representations using MLPs and differentiable volume rendering [S2][S3, S4, unofficial]. Prior to this, methods like Neural Volumes (Mildenhall et al., 2018) explored explicit voxel grids with learned warp fields for dynamic scenes [S1, unofficial][S2]. The field has since expanded to include explicit representations like 3D Gaussian Splatting (Kerbl et al., 2023) and Plenoxels, as well as specialized applications in medical imaging (EndoNeRF) and non-line-of-sight imaging (NeTF) [S2][S6, unofficial][S7]. The core insight is that the volume rendering equation is differentiable with respect to scene parameters, allowing gradient-based optimization to reconstruct 3D scenes from 2D images [S2].
+Radiance-field optimization emerged as a dominant paradigm for novel view synthesis following the introduction of Neural Radiance Fields (NeRF) by Mildenhall et al. in 2020 [S2]. Prior to this, image-based rendering relied on light-field sampling or explicit geometry reconstruction [S2]. NeRF demonstrated that a continuous volumetric function, optimized via gradient descent to minimize photometric error, could synthesize photorealistic images from sparse views [S1, unofficial][S5][S10]. This approach leveraged the differentiability of the volume rendering equation, allowing gradients to flow from pixel space to the neural network weights [44-radiance-field-optimization.md]. The field has since expanded to include explicit representations like voxel grids [4694-direct-voxel-grid-optimization.md] and Gaussian splats [1795-3d-gaussian-splatting.md], as well as applications in dynamic scenes [912-neural-volumes.md], medical imaging [S5], and non-optical domains like radar [8904-radar-fields.md].
 
